hi there this is Steve Kaufman founder  of Lync continuing my series of seven  secrets to successful language learning  you'll remember the first one was to  spend the time the second one was to do  the things that you like to do in  language learning the third one was to  develop and improve your ability to  notice what's happening in the language  now the fourth one is focus on words  words vocabulary is much more important  than grammar and in fact if you learn  words if you have lots of words and if  you learn these words naturally through  lots of listening and reading the  grammar will eventually fall into place  nothing prevents you from occasionally  reviewing grammar rules in a small  grammar book it's probably a good thing  to do I do it but my major emphasis is  accumulating words and that's why I  blink are number 1 measurable is how  many words do you know no some people  say that the emphasis should be on  learning chunks of words in the form of  phrases that we can easily reproduce and  certainly this is a good thing to do but  the phrases themselves consist of words  so you still have to know what words  mean and the words you can't learn them  in isolation you have to learn them from  meaningful content and that's why again  at link when you save a word when I save  a word to a date my personal database at  link not only do I capture all the  phrases where that word occurs but the  next time that word appears in any new  text link it'll be highlighted in yellow  and so I'm constantly working to  increase my known words total at link  grammar which is nothing other than a  description of correct usage is  something that develops gradually if you  try to memorize the rules of grammar and  even if you were able to learn the rules  which I think is  very difficult to do without a  sufficient amount of the input but even  if you learned the rules if you didn't  have enough of a vocabulary to follow a  conversation in a particular area or to  express express your ideas then you  would not be able to say very much on  the other hand if you have a large  vocabulary you will be surprised and how  gradually and especially if you you've  seen these words in different phrases  you'll start to notice how naturally you  start to speak with the words in the  correct order and in the correct form so  once again focus your efforts on words  and there are a number of strategies  that can help you some people like to  use flashcards and there's Anki and  supermemo and other such methods out  there my own preference is to do a lot  of listening and reading and I will  focus my listening and reading and as I  said in a previous video I will all as  much as possible try to read what I'm  listening to and listen to what I'm  reading but I will also focus on one  author at a particular during a  particular period or on one subject area  which might be politics or history or  economics and what this does it ensures  that a certain range of words appear  more frequently so that because I'm  staying with one author Polestar for  example in Russian then I become  familiar with his vocabulary or if I am  listening to current events interviews  and I become very familiar with that  vocabulary and so occasionally I'll  change my emphasis change the author  change the subject area but always try  to focus for a certain period of time on  one particular area so that I get enough  repetition so that I can accumulate my  vocabulary so there you have it the  secret number four focus on words and  the rest of it will fall into place  thank you for listening
 

